While create a table use this statement:
CREATE TABLE test(id Int, data String) PARTITIONED BY (data)
When use the SparkBatchWrite write the data into iceberg like that:
String Base = "";

for( int i = 0; i < 5000; i ++ ) {
  Random rnd = new Random();
  char c = (char) (rnd.nextInt(26) + 'a');
  Base = Base + "(" + i + ", '" + c + "'),";
}

spark.sql("INSERT INTO " + CATALOG_DB_TABLE + " VALUES " + Base + "(1, 'a')");

The  String Base cannot guarantee data grouped,  so iceberg throw a IllegalStateException in this code:
if (completedPartitions.contains(key)) {
  // if rows are not correctly grouped, detect and fail the write
   PartitionKey existingKey = Iterables.find(completedPartitions, key::equals, null);
   LOG.warn("Duplicate key: {} == {}", existingKey, key);
  throw new IllegalStateException("Already closed files for partition: " + key.toPath());
}

Once #315 gets pushed we should expose a Spark API for the same
@rdblue  had thoughts  on how to expose such an API in #315

@prodeezy & @rdsr, I think this could be exposed using Spark's multi-part name format, like we do with metadata tables.
For example, to read the history table you use db.table.history. We could do the same thing with snapshot IDs for time-travel, like db.table.982135791981. And we could then use .. to denote a range, like db.table.982135791981..7159871509310. That would get passed to Iceberg as Identifier(Array("db", "table", "982135791981", ""), "7159871509310").

When trying to use the AvroSchemaUtil in an external project to convert an Iceberg Schema to an Avro schema we run into a problem where the type of the Avro schema objects being returned is org.apache.iceberg.shaded.org.apache.avro.Schema instead of org.apache.avro.Schema which in turn means they can't be used in any classes which expect the original Avro type. Here is some sample code which demonstrates the problem:
import static org.apache.iceberg.types.Types.NestedField.required;
import org.apache.iceberg.Schema;
import org.apache.iceberg.avro.AvroSchemaUtil;
import org.apache.iceberg.types.Types;

public class HiveCatalogTest {

  public void avroSchemaWrongType()  {
    Schema schema = new Schema(Types.StructType
        .of(required(1, "foo", Types.LongType.get()), required(2, "bar", Types.LongType.get()))
        .fields());
    org.apache.iceberg.shaded.org.apache.avro.Schema convert = AvroSchemaUtil.convert(schema, "test");
  }
}

This problem occurs because Avro's classes are shaded and relocated into the Iceberg jar file. One fix would be to stop doing this but it then couples Iceberg a bit more tightly to a specific Avro version. The alternative would be to make the above AvroSchemaUtil class less visible (e.g. private) if the intention is for it not to be used outside of Iceberg itself but it feels like this is removing a potentially useful feature.
My preference would be for the removal of the shading and relocating, I'm happy to attempt a PR to do this.
